<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk.Razor">

	<PropertyGroup>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
		<ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<Version>5.2.7</Version>
		<PackageReleaseNotes>v5.2.7 - Live-stream audio: a streaming resampler, a sparse mel filterbank, and the counters that found them. NEW StreamingResampler: rate conversion for a LIVE microphone stream whose output is BIT-IDENTICAL to a whole-buffer AudioPreprocessor.Resample. Resample renormalises the kernel where its window overruns the SIGNAL EDGE - correct for one clip, wrong applied per chunk of a stream, because it then declares every chunk boundary an edge. A microphone delivers a frame every ~10 ms, so that is a broadband tick 100 times a second, which is exactly what a voice-activity detector reports as speech; MediaInterop.FromAudioDataAsync(data, targetRate) was doing it on every frame, so any caller asking MediaStreamCapture for a target sample rate got it. FIX: the Whisper mel filterbank was applied densely - 80 mels x 3000 frames x 201 frequency bins, about 48 million multiply-adds on the single WASM thread - but a Slaney mel filter is a TRIANGLE that touches a handful of those bins and is exactly zero elsewhere, so roughly 95% of that work multiplied by zero and added it. Each mel now runs only over its non-zero span; bit-identical, since 0 * power == 0 and x + 0 == x exactly in IEEE 754. MEASURED in a browser demo: the CPU mel STFT fell from 4786 ms to 3236 ms of a transcription, and it is a FIXED cost because the audio is padded to a flat 30 s before any of it runs - which is why adding a voice-activity endpointer shortened the RECORDING from 30 s to 3.7 s without shortening the transcription. NEW GraphExecutor cumulative counters (CumulativeReset plus CumulativeRunCount/TotalMs/ReadbackCount/ReadbackMs/SyncDrainCount/SyncDrainMs): every LastRun* field is overwritten by the next RunAsync, and most real pipelines are not one call - Whisper is an encoder pass plus N decoder steps, ZipVoice is NumSteps Euler iterations - so reading LastRunTotalMs after a 14-second transcription reports the final decode step and makes it look like 40 ms, a measurement that invites a confident wrong conclusion. What they found is the opposite of the Silero VAD result: readbacks are 3% of a Whisper transcription and were 66% of a VAD frame, same backend and same executor. TranscriptionResult also carries MelTimeMs and ModelTimeMs, because the executor's counters cannot see work that never reaches the executor. Also: BufferPool.TraceRents (ML_TRACE_RENTS=1) logs every named rent for diffing capture/replay passes. Full six-backend PMT sweep green: 4899 passed, 0 failed, 58 skipped. See CHANGELOG.</PackageReleaseNotes>
		<GeneratePackageOnBuild>True</GeneratePackageOnBuild>
		<GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
		<EmbedAllSources>true</EmbedAllSources>
		<DebugType>Embedded</DebugType>
		<Title>SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML</Title>
		<Authors>LostBeard</Authors>
		<Description>Hardware-agnostic machine learning infrastructure for .NET. Implements high-performance neural network layers in C# that transpile to WebGPU, CUDA, OpenCL, and WebGL via SpawnDev.ILGPU. Optimized for Blazor WebAssembly and native GPU execution.</Description>
		<PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML</PackageProjectUrl>
		<PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
		<PackageLicenseFile>LICENSE.txt</PackageLicenseFile>
		<PackageIcon>icon-128.png</PackageIcon>
		<RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/LostBeard/SpawnDev.ILGPU.ML.git</RepositoryUrl>
		<RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
		<PackageTags>Blazor;BlazorWebAssembly;WebGPU;WebGL;WebAssembly;WebWorkers;GPGPU;ILGPU;ParallelProcessing;Cuda;OpenCL;ComputeShader;CrossPlatform;MachineLearning</PackageTags>
		<NoPackageAnalysis>true</NoPackageAnalysis>
		<EnableDefaultCompileItems>true</EnableDefaultCompileItems>
		<IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
		<LangVersion>latest</LangVersion>
		<AllowUnsafeBlocks>true</AllowUnsafeBlocks>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<None Include="Assets\icon-128.png" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\LICENSE.txt" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
		<None Include="..\README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.SpawnJS" Version="2.1.9" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.ILGPU" Version="5.2.5" />
		<PackageReference Include="SpawnDev.WebTorrent" Version="4.2.2" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<SupportedPlatform Include="browser" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="windows" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="linux" />
		<SupportedPlatform Include="macos" />
	</ItemGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<EmbeddedResource Include="Data\imagenet_classes.txt" />
	</ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The MIT English frontend. ZipVoice cannot speak English without a phonemizer - its shipped lexicon
      is Chinese-only - and the reference implementation reaches for espeak-ng (GPL-3) at that point.
      This is dependency-free and browser-capable, so it costs nothing to carry.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="..\SpawnDev.Phonemizer\SpawnDev.Phonemizer.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
